                "1": {
                    "init": "comment np.rot90 / np.rotate - turn an array by quarter turns",
                    "name": "comment",
                    "id": 274,
                    "position_x": 24,
                    "position_y": -4,
                    "width": 660,
                    "height": 42,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "text",
                            "value": "np.rot90 / np.rotate - turn an array by quarter turns",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "font size",
                            "value": "48",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "comment": "np.rot90 / np.rotate - turn an array by quarter turns"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 917,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 876,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Turns an array by quarter turns, like rotating a picture.\n\nnp.rot90 and np.rotate are two names for the same node.\n\nUse it to turn an image or a grid on its side, or to change which way a pattern faces. One quarter turn swaps the number of rows and columns: a 7 by 6 array becomes 6 by 7.\n\nWHICH WAY:\nWith the default axes, a positive k turns ANTICLOCKWISE as a heat_map draws the array (row 0 at the top): the top row becomes the left column. A negative k turns clockwise. k 2 is a half turn (the same as flipping both axes), k 4 is back where you started, and k 5 is the same as k 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.rot90 <k: int> <axis 1: int> <axis 2: int>\nnp.rotate <k: int> <axis 1: int> <axis 2: int>\nDefaults: k 1, axis 1 0, axis 2 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.rot90 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Each array that arrives is turned and sent; only this inlet makes the node send. A 1-D array is treated as a single row, so one quarter turn makes it a column (N rows of 1).\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many quarter turns. Changing it does NOT send by itself - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\naxis 1 / axis 2 (int boxes, also inlets):\nThe plane to turn in: the turn goes FROM axis 1 TOWARDS axis 2. Swapping them reverses the direction. For a 2-D array leave them at 0 and 1; they matter for 3-D arrays, where they choose which pair of axes turns. A negative axis counts from the end (-1 is the last), one past the last is treated as the last; if the two end up the same, nothing is sent (a message says so).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotated array:\nThe turned array. With an odd k its first two dimensions have swapped.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) turned once anticlockwise lies on its back, 6 by 7, its top bar now down the left side. np.shape shows the shapes swap. Step k and it keeps turning; set k to 2 and it stands on its head; type 1 into axis 1 and 0 into axis 2 and the same k turns it clockwise.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.flip mirrors along one axis. np.transpose swaps rows and columns, which is a flip across the diagonal rather than a turn. np.roll shifts with wrap-around. np.reshape changes the shape without moving anything.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 860,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Turns an array by quarter turns, like rotating a picture.\n\nnp.rot90 and np.rotate are two names for the same node.\n\nUse it to turn an image or a grid on its side, or to change which way a pattern faces. One quarter turn swaps the number of rows and columns: a 7 by 6 array becomes 6 by 7.\n\nWHICH WAY:\nWith the default axes, a positive k turns ANTICLOCKWISE as a heat_map draws the array (row 0 at the top): the top row becomes the left column. A negative k turns clockwise. k 2 is a half turn (the same as flipping both axes), k 4 is back where you started, and k 5 is the same as k 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.rot90 <k: int> <axis 1: int> <axis 2: int>\nnp.rotate <k: int> <axis 1: int> <axis 2: int>\nDefaults: k 1, axis 1 0, axis 2 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.rot90 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Each array that arrives is turned and sent; only this inlet makes the node send. A 1-D array is treated as a single row, so one quarter turn makes it a column (N rows of 1).\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many quarter turns. Changing it does NOT send by itself - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\naxis 1 / axis 2 (int boxes, also inlets):\nThe plane to turn in: the turn goes FROM axis 1 TOWARDS axis 2. Swapping them reverses the direction. For a 2-D array leave them at 0 and 1; they matter for 3-D arrays, where they choose which pair of axes turns. A negative axis counts from the end (-1 is the last), one past the last is treated as the last; if the two end up the same, nothing is sent (a message says so).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotated array:\nThe turned array. With an odd k its first two dimensions have swapped.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) turned once anticlockwise lies on its back, 6 by 7, its top bar now down the left side. np.shape shows the shapes swap. Step k and it keeps turning; set k to 2 and it stands on its head; type 1 into axis 1 and 0 into axis 2 and the same k turns it clockwise.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.flip mirrors along one axis. np.transpose swaps rows and columns, which is a flip across the diagonal rather than a turn. np.roll shifts with wrap-around. np.reshape changes the shape without moving anything."
                },
